1
00:00:02,480 --> 00:00:03,360
Welcome to Launched.

2
00:00:03,520 --> 00:00:09,920
I'm Charlie Chapman, and today I'm excited to bring you the developer behind the wonderful recipe app Crouton, Devin Davies.

3
00:00:24,320 --> 00:00:25,520
Devin, welcome to the show.

4
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:26,800
Thanks for having me.

5
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:29,120
I am super excited about this.

6
00:00:30,480 --> 00:00:36,640
People who know me know that I spent a little while kind of just going through a lot of recipe apps.

7
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:46,000
Probably a lot of people I'm sure we'll get into this, but I came off of one app that's been around for a very long time and then maybe was ready to try some other things.

8
00:00:46,240 --> 00:00:48,320
And it turns out there's a bunch of really cool apps.

9
00:00:48,480 --> 00:00:49,600
I've interviewed some of them here.

10
00:00:50,160 --> 00:00:53,520
Crouton is one that I kind of fell in love with.

11
00:00:54,480 --> 00:01:01,600
There's a lot of little details that I'm sure we're going to get into that I just find very, very delightful in a way that's very satisfying.

12
00:01:01,840 --> 00:01:06,320
And so I'm, I'm really happy to have you on finally to kind of talk about this.

13
00:01:06,720 --> 00:01:07,440
I appreciate that.

14
00:01:07,600 --> 00:01:07,920
Thank you.

15
00:01:08,720 --> 00:01:12,960
So before we get into Crouton itself though, I want to give everyone a background on who you are.

16
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:16,720
So the first question or the questions I always ask everyone is where are you from?

17
00:01:17,280 --> 00:01:19,600
Do you have a formal education related to what you do?

18
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:22,960
And then we can talk about your career up to Crouton.

19
00:01:23,360 --> 00:01:23,520
Sure.

20
00:01:23,840 --> 00:01:25,920
So, I'm from Christchurch, New Zealand.

21
00:01:26,080 --> 00:01:27,760
You might be able to tell by the accent.

22
00:01:28,640 --> 00:01:37,120
I grew up in a smaller town outside of Christchurch called Kyopoi, which probably no one has heard of, but they have more cafes per capita than any other town in the world, I'm sure.

23
00:01:37,600 --> 00:01:46,080
I'm just assuming it's, it looks exactly like Lord of the Rings, but with more cafes and Taeko Waititi running around.

24
00:01:47,200 --> 00:01:47,520
That's right.

25
00:01:47,680 --> 00:01:47,760
Yeah.

26
00:01:48,320 --> 00:01:49,360
We take boat everywhere.

27
00:01:50,320 --> 00:01:52,400
I threw in all my stereo, all my knowledge.

28
00:01:52,480 --> 00:01:53,520
I'll just throw it in there at once.

29
00:01:53,680 --> 00:01:54,000
Oh, and sheep.

30
00:01:54,240 --> 00:01:55,600
There's lots of sheep, right?

31
00:01:55,840 --> 00:01:56,800
Yeah, plenty of sheep.

32
00:01:57,040 --> 00:01:58,480
Yeah, so it's it's a good place.

33
00:01:58,560 --> 00:02:00,800
It's pretty like a terrible holiday destination.

34
00:02:01,440 --> 00:02:03,840
If you're coming to New Zealand, don't come to Christchurch.

35
00:02:04,320 --> 00:02:04,880
Oh, why is that?

36
00:02:05,280 --> 00:02:06,080
It's a great place to live.

37
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:07,200
Oh, there's just nothing to do.

38
00:02:07,440 --> 00:02:09,280
It's very, it's flat and quiet.

39
00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:14,080
So if you want a relaxing, chill holiday, then maybe it's a good place, and you like coffee.

40
00:02:14,240 --> 00:02:20,800
Yeah, if you want to see me, then it's it's literally the best place in the world for that.

41
00:02:22,720 --> 00:02:25,520
Yeah, yeah, you'd be, you'd be hard pressed to find me anywhere else.

42
00:02:26,480 --> 00:02:28,320
But it's a good landing pad.

43
00:02:28,560 --> 00:02:32,000
We're pretty close to like Queenstown and Lake Ticapo and all these tourist destinations.

44
00:02:32,240 --> 00:02:35,680
So you normally fly into Christchurch and then drive out as quickly as you can.

45
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:39,840
But then that gives you, you know, quick access to them for like a weekend trip or something like that.

46
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:40,640
Yeah, that's true.

47
00:02:40,800 --> 00:02:44,080
Definitely very lucky to have lots of super nice spots close by.

48
00:02:44,560 --> 00:02:45,440
So it's not all bad.

49
00:02:45,920 --> 00:02:46,160
Nice.

50
00:02:46,560 --> 00:02:48,080
Let's talk about like your education then.

51
00:02:48,320 --> 00:02:52,320
Did you decide early on you wanted to get into app development or something like that?

52
00:02:52,960 --> 00:02:59,200
So originally I when I was a teenager, I my dad's work had an old C6 Photoshop license.

53
00:02:59,280 --> 00:03:00,160
They didn't need it anymore.

54
00:03:00,480 --> 00:03:04,960
So I got a legal copy of all of the creative suite.

55
00:03:05,040 --> 00:03:07,840
And so I started I was kind of into photography originally.

56
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:12,880
And then that kind of morphed into just using Photoshop to do a whole bunchch of different vector things.

57
00:03:13,680 --> 00:03:16,320
And so I thought for a while that I wanted to be a graphic designer.

58
00:03:16,480 --> 00:03:20,720
I don't know what it's like in the US though, but I think here that can be a tougher industry to get into.

59
00:03:20,960 --> 00:03:21,200
Oh yeah.

60
00:03:21,360 --> 00:03:22,960
To do well in, yeah.

61
00:03:23,840 --> 00:03:30,240
And so my dad who's a software developer kind of, I guess, sort of suggested that maybe I'd give this a go.

62
00:03:31,360 --> 00:03:34,000
And I've always been, I guess, like, Apple obsessed.

63
00:03:34,320 --> 00:03:38,240
I remember unboxing an iMac, a pink iMac G3 at primary school.

64
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:40,800
And I think that was my first kind of interaction with an Apple product.

65
00:03:40,960 --> 00:03:43,920
And it's like, yeah, I like whoever made this.

66
00:03:45,040 --> 00:03:48,960
And so I think kind of, ever since then, I've like always been a fan of Apple.

67
00:03:48,960 --> 00:03:53,840
Like always, before they used to live stream the events, me and my brother would, we'd stay up all night for some reason.

68
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:58,880
And then at 5 am, which is when they start in New Zealand, we'd watch the live blogs and then go to school.

69
00:03:59,280 --> 00:04:00,800
Oh, those were good times.

70
00:04:01,200 --> 00:04:04,640
Those were fun ways to consume those keynote.

71
00:04:04,800 --> 00:04:08,160
That sounds weird saying it like that, but it definitely had a different feel to it.

72
00:04:08,400 --> 00:04:10,960
Yeah, yeah, you're getting it, I guess, through someone else's perspective.

73
00:04:11,040 --> 00:04:15,680
I don't know, it was always good to have a few tabs open in case one of them went down and you needed to jump between different.

74
00:04:16,160 --> 00:04:20,000
Well, that's I I've probably said it here before, but that's the original reason I joined Twitter.

75
00:04:20,480 --> 00:04:24,320
Because of all places, Woot, if you've ever heard of Woot, that was probably an American thing.

76
00:04:24,560 --> 00:04:25,680
It was I think so.

77
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:39,680
One of those daily deals sites, but their whole shtick was they wrote like really fun, like they had comedians on staff to write these crazy descriptions of whatever their deal of the day was.

78
00:04:40,240 --> 00:04:46,320
And for some reason, Woot would live blog Apple keynotes on Twitter specifically.

79
00:04:46,960 --> 00:04:51,440
And on Twitter, you could sign up to have it, you know, send them as text messages to your phone.

80
00:04:52,080 --> 00:05:01,920
And when I learned that they did that, I was like, oh, while I'm in class, this was when I was in college, I can get those on my phone and I can keep up with the live blog.

81
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:02,880
But they're making jokes.

82
00:05:02,960 --> 00:05:04,000
Like it's literally just jokes.

83
00:05:04,480 --> 00:05:07,840
But I could at least be like, Oh, they announced a new laptop or they announced a new thing.

84
00:05:08,320 --> 00:05:09,760
You get a rough idea of what's happening.

85
00:05:09,920 --> 00:05:10,400
Yeah, yeah.

86
00:05:10,560 --> 00:05:15,200
So it was like the weirdest way to keep up with a keynote, but, uh, but there you go.

87
00:05:15,360 --> 00:05:16,000
So interesting.

88
00:05:16,480 --> 00:05:17,760
That's why I started on Twitter.

89
00:05:18,320 --> 00:05:19,840
Yeah, I think it would be hard to go back now.

90
00:05:20,080 --> 00:05:26,160
Now that we've got the live streams and especially the super polished video events, going back to just like a text feed, I think I'd probably struggle.

91
00:05:26,320 --> 00:05:28,480
But back in the day, it was like highlight of the year.

92
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:29,600
Oh, yeah, yeah.

93
00:05:30,320 --> 00:05:33,440
So you went into, did you go to university then for this?

94
00:05:33,920 --> 00:05:39,680
Yeah, so from after high school, I went to Canterbury University and got a computer science degree.

95
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:43,600
In retrospect, I don't know that you like, you definitely don't like need to do that.

96
00:05:43,760 --> 00:05:46,800
There's a lot of stuff I think that I had to learn that hasn't been that useful.

97
00:05:47,040 --> 00:05:50,560
But it also gives you a good idea of, I guess, the deeper levels of the way things work.

98
00:05:50,720 --> 00:05:57,920
So you can make some better decisions, even though most of the time when we're programming we're sort of at a much higher level these days than some of the things they teach you at university.

99
00:05:58,080 --> 00:06:08,800
It's, I guess it's good to have an appreciation of those things, but I definitely felt like for the first two years of uni, when it was a lot of that theory that I really just didn't have a clue what I was doing.

100
00:06:09,360 --> 00:06:13,840
Like I could do enough to pass the papers and things, but it didn't really click.

101
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:16,400
And I was sort of unsure if I'd, if I'd made the right call.

102
00:06:16,960 --> 00:07:01,680
Yeah, it's sort of a weird thing with college versions of computer science where it's like and maybe this is just people like us who are more into the well like you said the graphic design side of things but like building apps is like fun like I know people who do like those swift UI tutorial things and they're not planning to make a career out of it they just like it's kind of an addicting fun thing but I never once in college until maybe like later when I was doing senior project type stuff got into that like addicting flow with because it was all like learn how to you know do this database query and make it you know this time this one out faster or deal with big O notation or whatever.

103
00:07:01,840 --> 00:07:08,960
And it's like, those are probably good to have, but it would be nice, especially in the early stages, for it to have that, like, addicting piece.

104
00:07:09,520 --> 00:07:15,200
And then you love on the necessary, you know, your vegetables to go with the candy, I guess.

105
00:07:15,760 --> 00:07:16,080
That's right.

106
00:07:16,320 --> 00:07:16,400
Yeah.

107
00:07:16,480 --> 00:07:18,720
Because I, like, I just wanted to make buttons look nice.

108
00:07:18,880 --> 00:07:22,400
And so when you have to do all this extra stuff, it's a bit, a bit much.

109
00:07:23,040 --> 00:07:29,680
But in my third year, we had this, like, full year group project, uh, where we built an app to view the America's Cup, um, feed.

110
00:07:29,760 --> 00:07:31,200
Because they had it as a live API.

111
00:07:31,360 --> 00:07:34,320
So you could pull in the boats where they were and plot them on a map.

112
00:07:34,720 --> 00:07:35,520
The America's Cup.

113
00:07:35,920 --> 00:07:36,880
And then you said boats.

114
00:07:37,040 --> 00:07:37,760
See, I don't know.

115
00:07:37,840 --> 00:07:38,720
I don't even know what that is.

116
00:07:39,440 --> 00:07:40,080
Or maybe I do.

117
00:07:40,320 --> 00:07:41,040
Should I know what that is?

118
00:07:41,360 --> 00:07:42,640
It's got America in the title.

119
00:07:42,880 --> 00:07:43,040
I know.

120
00:07:45,040 --> 00:07:47,120
I wasn't aware of what it was until the group project.

121
00:07:47,600 --> 00:07:50,800
But now I'm vaguely aware that it involves boats and competition.

122
00:07:51,200 --> 00:07:52,800
Okay, some kind of race then, I guess.

123
00:07:53,120 --> 00:07:57,040
Yeah, they race boats, particular kind of boats around lakes.

124
00:07:57,520 --> 00:08:01,440
I'm going to do a terrible job of explaining what it is, because I just wanted to make buttons look pretty.

125
00:08:01,760 --> 00:08:06,320
In typical fashion, I've already derailed this into the least important part of this story.

126
00:08:07,520 --> 00:08:10,480
So you're making an app about the boats.

127
00:08:10,800 --> 00:08:11,520
Yes, that's right.

128
00:08:11,920 --> 00:08:13,200
Luckily, this will save it.

129
00:08:13,280 --> 00:08:18,240
In the middle of the year, we pivoted andted and turned it into a game because the America's Cup finished so the feeds stopped.

130
00:08:19,120 --> 00:08:23,360
And so then we changed it to be more like a, you know, the jackbox games?

131
00:08:23,760 --> 00:08:24,160
Yeah, yeah.

132
00:08:24,400 --> 00:08:34,880
Yeah, so we pretty much took that idea and made it so you could log into a room from your phone and that would give you a control pad and then you could pilote a boat around on the main screen and kind of race against your friends.

133
00:08:35,680 --> 00:08:36,480
Which was pretty cool.

134
00:08:37,120 --> 00:08:39,440
Wait, so that was, was that an iPhone app then?

135
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:42,240
It was just a web, a web page on the phone.

136
00:08:42,640 --> 00:08:43,680
Yeah, yeah, not an app.

137
00:08:43,920 --> 00:08:49,040
It was a, the actual application itself was just like a Java, was written in Java and ran on the Mac.

138
00:08:49,680 --> 00:08:49,920
Nice.

139
00:08:50,240 --> 00:08:57,040
Yeah, it was really that group project where I got very fortunate with the people who was on the team with me and learned like a whole bunch from that.

140
00:08:57,600 --> 00:08:59,600
And that was where it kind of, everything kind of fell into place.

141
00:08:59,680 --> 00:09:04,080
Like I had all the pieces from the previous years, but working on that with those people was when it kind of clicked.

142
00:09:04,160 --> 00:09:06,160
I was like, oh, this is what I want to be doing.

143
00:09:06,640 --> 00:09:10,000
And I got to do a lot of the design work on that project too, which was pretty cool.

144
00:09:10,400 --> 00:09:10,640
Nice.

145
00:09:10,960 --> 00:09:14,240
So you graduated with a computer science degree then.

146
00:09:14,800 --> 00:09:22,960
Coming out of that, what was your, like, did you go straight into app development or were there some, you know, twists through some other languages or something?

147
00:09:23,440 --> 00:09:27,680
I think I've been very fortunate with kind of the way things have fallen into place for me.

148
00:09:28,160 --> 00:09:30,320
Basically, I finished my degree and then.

149
00:09:30,320 --> 00:09:37,600
And then I spent I think I downloaded Xcode in my sort of gap between finding a job and kind of spent a week or two just mucking around.

150
00:09:38,080 --> 00:09:39,840
I didn't really know what I was doing then either.

151
00:09:40,080 --> 00:09:45,600
I just remember dragging buttons from the storyboard into the view controller and making it print out things when you tapped on them.

152
00:09:45,840 --> 00:09:47,440
There's a through line with buttons here.

153
00:09:47,680 --> 00:09:48,320
You like buttons.

154
00:09:49,280 --> 00:09:50,000
Just love buttons.

155
00:09:50,720 --> 00:09:51,680
Big, big button fan.

156
00:09:55,360 --> 00:09:56,720
Well, they're just good to fidget with.

157
00:09:56,880 --> 00:10:02,480
Like, I'm like, I still miss 3D touch because it was just a great kind of fidget when you were sitting there just kind of pressing on the buttons.

158
00:10:02,720 --> 00:10:08,400
I can hear Jordan Morgan, like, just sighing through the podcast pipes.

159
00:10:09,120 --> 00:10:09,920
What do you call that?

160
00:10:10,240 --> 00:10:10,240
Yeah.

161
00:10:11,680 --> 00:10:12,800
The old podcast pipes.

162
00:10:12,960 --> 00:10:13,040
Yeah.

163
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:14,480
They're gone.

164
00:10:14,960 --> 00:10:21,680
And so I interviewed for a few jobs and there was this company I had randomly come across online called Smudge.

165
00:10:22,880 --> 00:10:24,640
But they didn't have any like applications at the time.

166
00:10:24,800 --> 00:10:30,880
And so I applied for this other job and sort of went through the process of that but then didn't end up getting that role.

167
00:10:31,040 --> 00:10:34,240
And the recruiter that was helping me was like, Oh, why don't you apply at this company?

168
00:10:34,560 --> 00:10:38,000
And it just happened to be Smudge, the web, the company that I looked at online.

169
00:10:38,160 --> 00:10:39,600
I thought, Oh, it'd be cool to work there.

170
00:10:40,320 --> 00:10:45,920
And so I interviewed there and got the job and started that sort of after my summer break.

171
00:10:46,240 --> 00:10:47,920
And that was doing iOS development.

172
00:10:48,160 --> 00:10:52,320
So I kind of just landed straight into iOS development without any real experience.

173
00:10:52,800 --> 00:10:57,280
But then, yeah, just very fortunate to kind of learn as I was working.

174
00:10:57,680 --> 00:11:01,120
Okay, so you've been doing this pretty much your entire professional career then?

175
00:11:01,680 --> 00:11:07,200
Yeah, I've done a little bit of Android and web, but iOS is definitely the one I find the most exciting.

176
00:11:07,760 --> 00:11:12,560
So then what is what got you and you still you still work there then, right?

177
00:11:12,880 --> 00:11:13,440
Is that correct?

178
00:11:13,920 --> 00:11:14,800
If you want to talk about that?

179
00:11:15,520 --> 00:11:20,720
Yeah, yeah, so I still work there mostly full time and then yeah, just do Crouton on the side.

180
00:11:21,040 --> 00:11:31,440
So you end up it can be quite a good like, I think, relationship when you've got a side project and a work project that are the same technologies, you can learn things at home that are really helpful at work and vice versa.

181
00:11:31,680 --> 00:11:40,640
So you can almost like hack your, I don't know, like the whole ten thousand hours thing, you can kind of like fast track that if you're passionate about doing it in your spare time.

182
00:11:40,960 --> 00:11:53,040
That was I again, I've probably said this before on this podcast, but with Dark Noise, that was the whole point of it is I switched, you know, from web react side of things to an iOS team, but I didn't, I didn't know iOS development.

183
00:11:53,120 --> 00:11:54,800
So I'm like, I need to start a side project.

184
00:11:55,200 --> 00:12:05,600
And part of that too was all the people on my team or not all, but a lot of them also had side apps and the amount of times we'd be in a meeting and we'd be like in a meeting and we'd be like, can we do this with App Store Connect?

185
00:12:05,840 --> 00:12:08,000
And everyone would be like, well, I've done this here.

186
00:12:08,240 --> 00:12:09,440
Oh, I've used this over here.

187
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:14,960
Like everyone could kind of pull in this experience with these little side apps that they had.

188
00:12:15,680 --> 00:12:17,920
And I definitely found the same thing for me.

189
00:12:18,080 --> 00:12:25,600
It was like almost addicting when you could pull out your laptop, like your personal laptop, and be like, I solved this problem earlier.

190
00:12:25,760 --> 00:12:26,880
And you can find some old code.

191
00:12:27,360 --> 00:12:27,680
Totally.

192
00:12:28,160 --> 00:12:39,360
I never really had that doing.NET enterprise apps because I was not usually building those and deploying them to local enterprise apps.

193
00:12:40,960 --> 00:12:42,240
Not how you want to spend your evenings.

194
00:12:42,640 --> 00:12:44,000
Yeah, yeah, yeah, exactly.

195
00:12:44,320 --> 00:13:04,800
Yeah, I think as well, especially around like dub dub when all the new APIs and things come out, you don't always get an opportunity straight away through like a day job to use those things, but like when you're just on your own projects, you can do whatever you want and you don't have to sort of, I guess, justify it or have a reason to, and so you can pick up those things really quickly and then often they do come in useful later on down the line.

196
00:13:05,280 --> 00:13:08,640
So is that why you started building stuff on the side?

197
00:13:09,200 --> 00:13:15,680
I think once I started doing iOS development at work, I just found that I enjoyed it so much that I just just wanted to keep doing it when I got home.

198
00:13:16,160 --> 00:13:18,640
And I guess I've always had like.

199
00:13:19,280 --> 00:13:21,600
I know there's like pain points of the way that you might be doing something.

200
00:13:21,760 --> 00:13:28,640
And so when you suddenly develop a skill where you can solve those pain points yourself, it's kind of hard not to at least give it a try.

201
00:13:29,600 --> 00:13:41,840
Yeah, it's the, like, I'm having a little problem with my garage door and I like Raspberry Pies, therefore, I need some giant Rube Goldberg machine that attaches a Raspberry Pie to my garage door.

202
00:13:42,400 --> 00:13:44,160
It's the same kind of problem, but with apps.

203
00:13:44,480 --> 00:13:48,000
I think the problem is not having someone there to tell you that you should just stop.

204
00:13:48,400 --> 00:13:48,480
Yeah.

205
00:13:48,720 --> 00:13:50,480
And just enjoy some peace and quiet.

206
00:13:51,360 --> 00:13:58,000
I guess you had you had a full-time job and you were just kind of like making apps for fun., I guess on the side.

207
00:13:58,240 --> 00:14:08,080
Were those were you doing apps like pushing them to the store and trying to make kind of a business out of them or were most of them sort of personal side project kind of things at before Crouton?

208
00:14:08,720 --> 00:14:11,280
Yeah, it was all mostly personal, I guess you would say.

209
00:14:11,520 --> 00:14:17,920
It was so the first app I built was for my youth group just as a way to keep track of the different events we had on.

210
00:14:18,160 --> 00:14:22,640
Because often you'd have like a printed plan, but then things would change and the printed plan would be kind of useless.

211
00:14:22,880 --> 00:14:23,040
Right.

212
00:14:23,280 --> 00:14:30,720
And so the first app I built was just something you could open up and see what was happening that week and it would change as things updated.

213
00:14:31,280 --> 00:14:39,440
I think in retrospect that could have probably just been a Google calendar, but it was a good one opportunity to practice building my own thing and seeing what that might look like.

214
00:14:40,240 --> 00:14:43,360
So what led you up to Crouton itself then?

215
00:14:43,760 --> 00:14:44,720
I guess kind of similar.

216
00:14:44,960 --> 00:14:51,440
When I, my wife and I got married, we started meal planning just as a way to keep groceries cheap.

217
00:14:51,680 --> 00:14:54,880
And it also just makes it easy when you're working out what you want to cook.

218
00:14:55,200 --> 00:14:56,560
I've always enjoyed cooking.

219
00:14:56,800 --> 00:15:00,640
I don't like, I don't think I'm particularly amazing at it, but it's something I've always kind of enjoyed.

220
00:15:00,880 --> 00:15:03,360
So I was the, the, the meal planner in the house.

221
00:15:03,520 --> 00:15:04,560
That's my official title.

222
00:15:04,800 --> 00:15:06,880
And to start with, I was just using the notes app.

223
00:15:07,200 --> 00:15:08,960
So at the top, I just had the days of the week.

224
00:15:09,200 --> 00:15:12,560
And then, under that, I had a, just a massive list.

225
00:15:13,040 --> 00:15:15,680
of URLs and even some typed out recipes.

226
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:23,280
So you kind of just infinite scroll, find something that looks good and then paste it up to the top, which worked surprisingly well for a while.

227
00:15:23,920 --> 00:15:26,880
Like you get iCloud sharing in a note so you can share it with your family.

228
00:15:27,200 --> 00:15:27,680
Yeah, yeah.

229
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:29,920
I think the Notes app in general is great.

230
00:15:30,080 --> 00:15:36,080
There's a lot of like app ideas that you can kind of manage for a while just as a note until they become weirdly.

231
00:15:36,480 --> 00:15:42,160
If we get links to other notes inside Apple Notes, it's going to be a killer app.

232
00:15:42,320 --> 00:15:46,640
I mean, it's a killer app now, but like I feel like that's kind of the one piece missing.

233
00:15:46,960 --> 00:15:48,000
It'll put us all out of work.

234
00:15:48,400 --> 00:15:48,480
Yeah.

235
00:15:49,040 --> 00:15:50,480
Yeah, you can just use Notes for that.

236
00:15:51,200 --> 00:15:55,040
And so I don't really remember making a conscious decision to start making crouton.

237
00:15:55,120 --> 00:15:56,240
I think it was just one Saturday.

238
00:15:56,560 --> 00:16:02,400
I had some spare time and just sort of started putting something together to see what it might look like.

239
00:16:03,680 --> 00:16:14,640
And originally it was really focused around, I guess coming from the note where I just had links and recipes, it was actually really focused on the meal planning and less about being able to view recipes in the app.

240
00:16:14,880 --> 00:16:21,120
And so originally it really was just like a you would just paste links in and then you could attach those links to the days of the week.

241
00:16:21,440 --> 00:16:28,640
So I guess the benefit was that you could search it and things like that, but it really didn't have any focus on being able to do anything interesting with the recipes.

242
00:16:29,040 --> 00:16:32,560
Like when you wanted to use one, you're just opening up that page in the browser.

243
00:16:32,960 --> 00:16:40,640
Basically, yeah, just like Safari View Controller, and then there it was, which I don't, I don't, like, I don't think I shipped that version of it, but that's what it was for quite a while.

244
00:16:40,880 --> 00:16:54,880
And then I think as I went on, it was like, okay, I'm going to have to actually display ingredients and steps and figure out how to make these things look sensible and all the sort of, I guess, complications that you don't think will be issues until you start trying to build it.

245
00:16:55,760 --> 00:17:01,280
And like at this point in the story, it's more or less for you still then, right?

246
00:17:02,640 --> 00:17:07,760
Like when did you kind of transition to like, oh, this is going to be like a product I'm going to try and sell.

247
00:17:09,040 --> 00:17:11,760
That was probably definitely after it launched, I think.

248
00:17:12,320 --> 00:17:13,520
Oh, after it launched?

249
00:17:14,560 --> 00:17:16,720
Well, I guess we can get into the launch soon.

250
00:17:16,880 --> 00:17:20,560
But yeah, while I was building it, it was definitely like, this is going to make things easier for me.

251
00:17:20,800 --> 00:17:24,000
I guess always you're sort of thinking like, oh, it would be nice if other people used this.

252
00:17:24,160 --> 00:17:26,560
But I don't think I had really thought about that too much.

253
00:17:27,120 --> 00:17:27,200
Okay.

254
00:17:27,760 --> 00:17:35,920
It wasn't like grand plans about, you know, taking over the recipe app space and doing market research and, you know, all that.

255
00:17:36,160 --> 00:17:42,720
It was really like, this is a thing that will be nice for me and it'll be cool like a lot of projects to put it out there.

256
00:17:42,880 --> 00:17:46,240
Like if it was a website, it'd be cool to put it out out there and tweet about it or something.

257
00:17:47,440 --> 00:17:47,920
Yeah, yeah.

258
00:17:48,080 --> 00:17:50,720
I definitely didn't do any like product market fit or anything like that.

259
00:17:50,800 --> 00:17:52,480
I was just like, oh, this would be good for me.

260
00:17:52,720 --> 00:17:57,120
And I I had done a search in the app store, um, just to kind of see what was around.

261
00:17:57,680 --> 00:18:05,840
Um, obviously, like, Paprika kind of owns the space, did and still does in a lot of ways, and I think at the time, I just didn't want to pay eight dollars.

262
00:18:06,320 --> 00:18:12,000
Well, in retrospect, that probably would have been a good deal and would have saved me a lot of, um, stress and heartbreak.

263
00:18:12,160 --> 00:18:26,560
But, um, but yeah, a lot of the meal planning apps, because that was kind of my angle were really focused on health and that side of it, rather than just the I just want to cook something tomorrow and I'm going to organise that at the start of the week.

264
00:18:27,280 --> 00:18:32,960
Originally in Crouton as well, you could only, you weren't assigning recipes to dates, you were just assigning them to the day of the week.

265
00:18:33,360 --> 00:18:35,440
So it was like really, really basic.

266
00:18:35,920 --> 00:18:40,080
Basically at the start of the next week, you'd have to clear the list and then add in the recipes again.

267
00:18:40,480 --> 00:18:40,960
Oh yeah.

268
00:18:41,920 --> 00:18:42,240
Interesting.

269
00:18:42,480 --> 00:18:48,480
So there's like a lot of decisions I made that I think made it clear that it was built just for the way that I used recipes apps.

270
00:18:48,560 --> 00:19:00,000
And then over time, as people have actually started using it, then I've sort of tried to, I guess, soften some of those things, like still have an opinion and the way things should be done, but also take feedback and sort of refine things a bit.

271
00:19:01,200 --> 00:19:08,320
So based on how you are describing it, I'm guessing there wasn't a big beta test group or anything like that.

272
00:19:08,960 --> 00:19:13,760
What was the state of the app and then like what was the actual launch itself like then, the initial launch?

273
00:19:14,160 --> 00:19:24,800
Yeah, I don't even know if I'm allowed to be on this show because I don't know if you can really call it a launch, but I Well, it went from existing to not existing, so the boat ended up in the water, I think that's Yes, yeah.

274
00:19:25,760 --> 00:19:33,520
So at that point I really I'd been on Twitter for a long time, but I actually didn't follow a lot of people in like the IOS developer space.

275
00:19:33,920 --> 00:19:38,800
And so I'd started tweeting about it a little bit, but I actually checked earlier today, a little before Carl.

276
00:19:38,800 --> 00:19:41,040
today before coming on to see what I had done up to it.

277
00:19:41,120 --> 00:19:47,360
And I think I tweeted once, showing a little video of it automatically detecting something on your clipboard popping up.

278
00:19:48,240 --> 00:19:50,240
Another screenshot of it running on Catalyst.

279
00:19:50,400 --> 00:19:52,480
And then the post after that was the launch tweet.

280
00:19:53,200 --> 00:19:58,080
And so there was really like no kind of, I guess, like, you know, trying to build up hype or see if there's any interest.

281
00:19:58,320 --> 00:20:00,880
It was kind of just like, hey Ron, I made a recipe app.

282
00:20:01,120 --> 00:20:01,760
And that was kind of it.

283
00:20:02,160 --> 00:20:05,760
And the screenshot of it was not very thought through.

284
00:20:05,920 --> 00:20:12,800
It was like a recipe that I just quickly typed up, which had like the ingredients was a chicken breast and two tablespoons of curry powder.

285
00:20:13,040 --> 00:20:14,320
And that was like the screenshot.

286
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:16,160
Yeah.

287
00:20:16,160 --> 00:20:21,760
But yeah, I clearly wasn't thinking at all about how to present this as something that other people might want to use.

288
00:20:22,240 --> 00:20:36,000
It's so interesting because I came into understanding or knowledge of this later, but everything you do now feels like very thought through and there's little jokes hidden in places and I feel like you do really think through that.

289
00:20:36,240 --> 00:20:41,360
So I'm curious how you like, how you got from there to where you are right now then?

290
00:20:42,160 --> 00:20:43,920
Yeah, I think I'm making up for lost time.

291
00:20:45,040 --> 00:20:52,880
Because basically it launched in 2019 and it got, I think, two downloads on the first day, which was me and my mom.

292
00:20:53,360 --> 00:20:54,720
So it went very well.

293
00:20:55,360 --> 00:20:56,240
She thought it was a good app.

294
00:20:57,040 --> 00:20:59,920
A small but very loyal user base, I think is what it's called.

295
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:00,560
Exactly, yeah.

296
00:21:00,640 --> 00:21:03,120
Well, she still uses it, so it's That's awesome.

297
00:21:03,360 --> 00:21:04,320
Must have done it right.

298
00:21:04,720 --> 00:21:11,120
So it was actually, I think, almost a whole year or at least six to eight months where no one was really using it, but I was still working on it.

299
00:21:11,200 --> 00:21:14,960
It was good at least that it had solved a problem for me and was legitimately useful.

300
00:21:15,440 --> 00:21:20,240
Was it paid upfront or in-app purchase, which is what I think it is today?

301
00:21:20,640 --> 00:21:26,480
Yeah, I think when I launched it was free and then there was in-app purchase to unlock iCloud sync.

302
00:21:26,720 --> 00:21:26,720
Okay.

303
00:21:27,280 --> 00:21:34,480
That didn't do very well, and so then after a few months I changed it to paid upfront, which also just made it easierier to develop because I didn't have to worry about it.

304
00:21:34,880 --> 00:21:34,960
Yeah.

305
00:21:35,520 --> 00:21:49,440
You get through app reviews so much faster and it's funny, I mean, by the time this comes out, it'll be old, but I've been tweeting, I think today or last night, I don't remember, about thinking about changing things with Dark Noise.

306
00:21:49,680 --> 00:21:56,080
And it's one of those where it's like, it's exciting thinking about it, but man, paid upfront is so much easier.

307
00:21:56,320 --> 00:22:04,720
Like as much as there's a reason why everybody says not to do it, I understand that from a business sense, but you avoid so much work.

308
00:22:05,840 --> 00:22:06,240
Definitely.

309
00:22:06,560 --> 00:22:07,040
Surprisingly.

310
00:22:07,680 --> 00:22:10,640
There's definitely a lot of positives about paid upfront, but it's hard to get people to.

311
00:22:10,640 --> 00:22:12,240
to get people to convince people to download it.

312
00:22:12,400 --> 00:22:18,000
Yeah, you're asking people to put a lot of money down on something or not a lot, but you know what I mean?

313
00:22:18,240 --> 00:22:24,720
Put money down on something based on no real reputation, just what they can see in screenshots.

314
00:22:24,960 --> 00:22:29,360
And you're up against a bunch of things that usually they have to put no money down to try it out.

315
00:22:30,080 --> 00:22:30,160
Yeah.

316
00:22:30,800 --> 00:23:34,720
And I think so switching to paid up front was also not the right move because then it was basically like direct comparison to Paprika and other apps where I just like I'm amazed at how little the app did when I shipped it that I like in retrospect I probably should have I think held off for a few extra months and just like tidied up a few extra things and and launched it then i think it was just good to get something out the door and start using it and i think what happened after that was i started really getting into the developer community a lot more and realizing that there's a lot of other people trying to do very similar things and uh so i just started yeah started getting really inspired about all the different things you see people working on on twitter and also looking at how they i guess market their new features and their their updates and post about things and i guess try and analyze what works and what doesn't work and so i think yeah now when i post things i'm definitely thinking about that um a lot more probably too much at this point but just around like, I don't know, trying to get the perfect screen recording of a feature or exactly how something's going to look when someone else views it without any context and things like that.

317
00:23:35,040 --> 00:23:44,960
So a lot of that I think I've just built up from looking at how other people do things and kind of trying to, I guess, copy what I think works well and trying to do it, I guess, in my own way too.

318
00:23:45,200 --> 00:23:47,600
But yeah, just looking at how other people are doing it.

319
00:23:48,560 --> 00:24:00,000
So I feel like, I mean, I don't know the reality, so maybe my perception is way off here, but I feel like there's more than two users, well, three, because I'm one of them using the app now.

320
00:24:00,320 --> 00:24:04,720
So like, what sort of changed the tide on something that was another app in the store?

321
00:24:05,600 --> 00:24:10,960
wasn't getting a lot of attention to something that seems like at least a genuine business.

322
00:24:12,320 --> 00:24:16,560
I think there was a release I put out in early 2020.

323
00:24:16,720 --> 00:24:19,280
It was like kind of after COVID hit and I was at home a lot more.

324
00:24:19,520 --> 00:24:23,760
And I think I just focused on all the kind of key points that I was getting.

325
00:24:24,320 --> 00:24:26,560
Like I was kind of getting a few users over time.

326
00:24:26,720 --> 00:24:28,640
And so I was starting to get a little bit of feedback.

327
00:24:29,280 --> 00:24:34,080
And so there was a release I put out that added like grocery planning and it made the meal plan infinite.

328
00:24:34,160 --> 00:24:36,640
So you could plan into the future as much as you want.

329
00:24:37,040 --> 00:24:44,240
And a few extra things in that release that I think kind of took it from just something that solved my problem to being legitimately useful for other people.

330
00:24:45,120 --> 00:24:49,760
And I definitely kind of see that as kind of a marker of where it established itself as an app.

331
00:24:50,400 --> 00:24:57,280
Was there like a notable maybe not jump is the wrong word, but growth that happened after that?

332
00:24:58,000 --> 00:25:00,640
I think most of Crouton's growth has been pretty gradual.

333
00:25:01,120 --> 00:25:02,560
There's definitely been like some spikes.

334
00:25:02,800 --> 00:25:10,080
Like I got picked up by Mac Stories late 2020, I think.

335
00:25:10,640 --> 00:25:15,280
And that also was a really great way to kind of just get out in front of other people and be seen.

336
00:25:15,520 --> 00:25:19,600
And so there's definitely been like some spikes where different things have happened, like app store features and things like that.

337
00:25:19,680 --> 00:25:26,800
But I would say on the whole, Croutons' growth has been really kind of progressive over time, which is like good and bad.

338
00:25:27,040 --> 00:25:30,960
Sometimes those big spikes can be a little bit, I guess, depressing when you come back down from them.

339
00:25:31,280 --> 00:25:36,240
You just want to chase the feeling of like, I don't know, massive growth overnight again.

340
00:25:36,320 --> 00:25:45,280
Whereas then when it's just kind of gradual over time, it's a little bit more sustainable and it's like, okay, if I just keep doing this and focusing on what I think makes a good product, then, you know, hopefully it will continue down that path.

341
00:25:45,920 --> 00:25:46,400
That's interesting.

342
00:25:46,640 --> 00:25:51,680
So did you find that after those spikes, the tail kind of continued?

343
00:25:51,920 --> 00:25:54,720
Like you got some growth out of that?

344
00:25:54,880 --> 00:26:03,200
Or were those really just like if you just got rid of those, yes., you'd lose the money from those spikes, but they didn't necessarily affect the overall trajectory of the app.

345
00:26:03,680 --> 00:26:14,000
Like how or was most of the growth, I guess, coming from the sort of day to day grinding or were those spikes the things that were kind of driving some of that growth, I guess is what I'm really asking.

346
00:26:14,640 --> 00:26:16,320
Yeah, I guess it's hard to know exactly.

347
00:26:16,640 --> 00:26:21,120
My I'd definitely be interested to see what other people, what their experience has been.

348
00:26:21,360 --> 00:26:29,760
But typically after those, I found that it levels off sort of a little bit above where it was before, but certainly not like that while it's happening, you're like, oh, this is awesome.

349
00:26:29,840 --> 00:26:32,000
I'm going to be like, you know, number one forever.

350
00:26:32,560 --> 00:26:40,880
Yeah, James Thompson, he was talking about when they released, like when the App Store first came out out, so there was no stories or history to go with.

351
00:26:41,600 --> 00:26:48,640
They all were projecting out like these numbers over the next however many years and they're like, Oh my gosh, we're gonna be like millionaires.

352
00:26:50,400 --> 00:26:51,520
Exactly, yeah, yeah.

353
00:26:51,760 --> 00:27:00,000
So I think, in my experience has been that after that, it levels off somewhere slightly above where it was previously, but not, not anything significant.

354
00:27:00,720 --> 00:27:13,360
And so I think for me, what's worked has been just over time, I think, building up a presence on Twitter and kind of just sharing what I'm working on and then just the consistency of delivering and trying to add new things that will be helpful.

355
00:27:14,160 --> 00:27:18,080
And over time, then, like, the word of mouth of your product spreads.

356
00:27:18,400 --> 00:27:19,920
And then it kind of builds up that way.

357
00:27:20,720 --> 00:27:22,080
At least that's been my experience.

358
00:27:22,800 --> 00:27:23,760
Yeah, that makes sense.

359
00:27:24,080 --> 00:27:26,000
Let's, I want to talk about a couple of those features.

360
00:27:26,240 --> 00:27:33,840
So, like, I guess one of the more standout ones is, uh, the blink, what do you, what do you actually call the feature?

361
00:27:33,920 --> 00:27:39,120
Uh, it's like blink detection for, uh, how you actually navigate while you're running through your steps.

362
00:27:39,680 --> 00:27:42,000
Yeah, I think it's just called, like, wink mode or hands free.

363
00:27:42,240 --> 00:27:42,640
Wink mode.

364
00:27:42,720 --> 00:27:43,840
That's, yeah, that's the word.

365
00:27:44,160 --> 00:27:45,040
How'd that come about?

366
00:27:45,840 --> 00:27:50,880
That one was interesting because, uh, like, I didn't have a step by step mode in Crouton at all before that.

367
00:27:51,200 --> 00:27:55,760
And then I think I was just driving home from work one day and I don't know, daydreaming or something.

368
00:27:55,920 --> 00:28:00,000
It's like, oh, it'd be cool if I could like move through the recipe without having to touch the screen.

369
00:28:00,640 --> 00:28:01,360
That would be kind of fun.

370
00:28:02,560 --> 00:28:05,040
And then I guess just thought, oh, how could I do that?

371
00:28:05,200 --> 00:28:08,320
And then, yeah, just thought about the TrueDepth camera.

372
00:28:08,320 --> 00:28:09,840
Oh, you don't even need the TrueDepth now.

373
00:28:09,920 --> 00:28:12,560
You just, just the front camera with the face detection.

374
00:28:13,360 --> 00:28:16,960
And then I just thought, oh, like, yeah, maybe I could detect if you're winking or opening your mouth.

375
00:28:17,040 --> 00:28:19,040
And that could be a way to move through the steps.

376
00:28:19,440 --> 00:28:21,280
But I really thought it was kind of like a gimmick.

377
00:28:21,440 --> 00:28:23,040
I wasn't sure if I was actually going to ship it.

378
00:28:23,280 --> 00:28:29,840
And it took like a week to get it through app review as well because they kept sending back feedback like, where are you using TrueDepth in your app?

379
00:28:30,320 --> 00:28:32,240
And it just took a very long time to explain.

380
00:28:32,400 --> 00:28:38,720
Like, some checkbox is checked and then they need to actually go find that which makes your app review slower, I guess.

381
00:28:38,880 --> 00:28:39,760
Probably every time now.

382
00:28:40,160 --> 00:28:43,440
I think at this point I've gotten on that feature specifically.

383
00:28:43,600 --> 00:28:48,480
I've worked out exactly what I need to include to kind of avoid having to answer questions about that one.

384
00:28:48,640 --> 00:28:54,320
But yeah, there's definitely some good videos of me like late at night winking at my phone to kind of submit as a proof.

385
00:28:56,480 --> 00:28:57,760
But those would never be distributed.

386
00:28:58,560 --> 00:29:07,040
So you were saying you weren't sure if that was going to be if you thought it was going to be a gimmick, but then Yeah, I mean, I think it probably is still a gimmick.

387
00:29:07,200 --> 00:29:08,000
I do use it occasionally.

388
00:29:08,720 --> 00:29:16,720
But it landed a lot better than I thought it was going to, because I just thought it was kind of like a goofy thing that would be fun to put together and see if I could make it work.

389
00:29:17,440 --> 00:29:30,400
But then almost every time now when it gets picked up by someone, they'd often mention that as like a key feature, which I just find amusing, because to me it was just like a little thing I kind of like threw in as something fun that I could do.

390
00:29:30,880 --> 00:29:36,880
We've talked outside of this podcast a couple times and you sometimes make fun of me for not using step by step mode.

391
00:29:37,520 --> 00:29:42,480
I will say, I do find myself now with using crouton.

392
00:29:43,040 --> 00:29:46,720
If I'm cooking certain things, like if I'm chopping vegetables or whatever and it's a recipe I.

393
00:29:47,520 --> 00:29:48,240
don't know as well.

394
00:29:48,960 --> 00:29:54,400
I will set up my phone because I don't use a kitchen iPad, which I know you're also a big proponent of.

